\begin{Example} Consider  $k h_i$, $k\in \mathbb{N}$ (i.e., the sum of $k$ identical signal functions). If $k=1$, then $h_i$ is robust if and only if $\bdry h_i$ has 0 or 2 elements (Lemma~\ref{l-element-counts-for-robustness}). If $k > 1$ then, unless $\card\bdry h_i=0$, perturbing the radius of just one signal function will always change $\card \bdry H_{k}(\cdot)$, thus changing the sequence representation; see Figure~\ref{fig:multiplicity}. Therefore, if $k >1$, $k h_i$ is robust if and only if $\card \bdry h_i=0$.

