%During accelerator operation, factors such as power supply fluctuations and ground vibrations can induce phase jitter in the RF cavities. For the SHINE facility, the rms phase jitter is specified as 0.01$^\circ$ for cavities L1 and L2, and 0.02$^\circ$ for cavity LH. Additionally, the amplitude jitter for all cavities is maintained at 0.01\%. To assess the impact of these jitters, 100 different jitter scenarios were simulated for each of the two cases under study, with the corresponding parameters illustrated in figure \ref{fig:rms}. The simulations reveal that, in both cases, a shorter FWHM width of the current spike correlates with a higher peak current. However, this increased peak current is accompanied by a larger slice energy spread at the peak. Notably, the tail-current spike case exhibits greater sensitivity to the specified jitter parameters compared to the head-current spike case.


\section{FEL simulation results}
To evaluate the performance of the achievable attosecond XFEL pulses under realistic accelerator conditions, we performed FEL simulations using GENESIS 1.3 (version 4) \cite{genesis}. These simulations incorporate 100 statistically varied electron beam distributions that account for expected rf phase and amplitude jitter in the linac sections shown in figure~\ref{fig:rms}. The results are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:head_power} and Figure~\ref{fig:tail_power}. These simulations are based on the SHINE FEL-1 parameters \cite{cwshine2024}, employing an undulator with a period of 26 mm and a length of 4 m to generate XFEL pulses with a photon energy of 6 keV. For both cases, a slight reserve taper was incorporated into the undulator K values to match the chirp of the electron beam. Since the radiation generated outside the main current spike is negligible compared to that from the high-current region, only the spike region is shown in the figures.

