At the end of the fourteenth undulator, the average FWHM pulse duration for the head-current spike case is 308 as $\pm$ 133 as, with the peak power of 791 GW $\pm$ 381 GW, demonstrating that the target attosecond performance is well maintained despite the presence of the jitter from the linac. For the tail-current spike case, the average pulse duration is 255 as $\pm$ 100 as, and the peak power is 522 GW $\pm$ 376 GW. All FWHM durations reported in this study refer to the envelope pulse duration, obtained via a global Gaussian fit to the radiation power profile. The head-current spike case produces, on average, both longer pulses and higher peak power, which can be attributed to its broader current profile and lower slice energy spread. Figure \ref{fig:head_power}c and \ref{fig:head_power}d present two typical pulses from the head-current spike case with a pulse duration of 343 as and 94 as, respectively.


In both head-current and tail-current spike cases, the majority of pulses remain well below 600 as in duration. Furthermore, a fraction of the simulated shots exhibit peak powers exceeding 1~TW, accompanied by pulse durations below 100 as, as shown in figure \ref{fig:head_power}d and figure \ref{fig:tail_power}d. For these cases, the FEL process shows continued pulse shortening after saturation, exhibiting a superradiant behavior\cite{bonifacio1989superradiance,soft5}. Moreover, it is worth noting that beyond the attosecond pulse generation presented here, the electron beam featuring a tail-current spike also holds potential for various advanced XFEL operation modes, including multi-stage amplification\cite{wang2024millijoule,soft1}, attosecond XFEL carrying orbital angular momentum\cite{yan2023self,xu2024fel}, and enhanced self-seeding scheme\cite{hemsing2020enhanced,liu2025generation}.

