\medskip
\noindent
{\bf Finding a sample-based EW D-optimal approximate design}
\smallskip

\noindent
Next, we look for an EW D-optimal approximate design rather than a locally D-optimal one with the prespecified  $\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}$. It is more robust against misspecified parameter values \citep{lin2025ew}. More specifically, we first draw $B=1,000$ bootstrapped samples from the previous data (Table~1 in \cite{atkinson1999}), and then obtain the corresponding parameter estimates $\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_j$ from the $j$th bootstrapped sample, for $j=1, \ldots, B$. 
The $1,000$ parameter vectors are stored as matrix \verb|theta_matrix| as below:

\begin{verbatim}
> n=1000
> set.seed(2024)
> tt1=rmultinom(n, size = 500, prob = c(62/500, 5/500, 433/500))
> tt1t=t(tt1)
> tt1x=cbind(80,6400,tt1t)
> tt2=rmultinom(n, size = 500, prob = c(94/500, 24/500, 382/500))
> tt2t=t(tt2)
> tt2x=cbind(100,10000,tt2t)
> tt3=rmultinom(n, size = 500, prob = c(179/500, 60/500,  261/500))
> tt3t=t(tt3)
> tt3x=cbind(120,14400,tt3t)
> tt4=rmultinom(n, size = 500, prob = c(335/500, 80/500, 85/500))
> tt4t=t(tt4)
> tt4x=cbind(140,19600,tt4t)
> tt5=rmultinom(n, size = 500, prob = c(432/500, 46/500,  22/500))
> tt5t=t(tt5)
> tt5x=cbind(160,25600,tt5t)
> tt6=rmultinom(n, size = 500, prob = c( 487/500, 11/500,  2/500))
> tt6t=t(tt6)
> tt6x=cbind(180,32400,tt6t)
> tt7=rmultinom(n, size = 500, prob = c(498/500, 2/500, 0/500))
> tt7t=t(tt7)
> tt7x=cbind(200,40000,tt7t)

> theta_matrix=rep(0,n*5); dim(theta_matrix)=c(n,5)
> for (i in 1:n) {
+ data = rbind(tt1x[i,], tt2x[i,], tt3x[i,], tt4x[i,], tt5x[i,], tt6x[i,],
+ tt7x[i,])
+ colnames(data)=c('x1','x2','y1','y2','y3')
+  data1=data.frame(data)
+  # assign(paste0('Simu',i),data.frame(data))
+  fit1 <- vglm(cbind(y1,y2,y3) ~ x1+x2, family=sratio, data1)
+  theta1=coef(fit1)
+  fit2 <- vglm(cbind(y1,y2,y3) ~ x1, family=sratio, data1)
+  theta2=coef(fit2)
+ theta_matrix[i,1]=data.matrix(theta1)[1]
+ theta_matrix[i,2]=data.matrix(theta1)[3]
+ theta_matrix[i,3]=data.matrix(theta1)[5]
+ theta_matrix[i,4]=data.matrix(theta2)[2]
+ theta_matrix[i,5]=data.matrix(theta2)[4]
}
\end{verbatim}

With the parameter matrix \verb|theta_matrix|, the functions \texttt{hfunc.temp} and \texttt{hprime.temp} previously defined, we use \verb|EW_ForLion_MLM_Optimal()| function to find a sample-based EW D-optimal approximate design as follows:

\begin{verbatim}
> set.seed(123)
> EW_forlion.design_MLM =EW_ForLion_MLM_Optimal(J=3 ,n.factor=c(0), 
+ factor.level=list(c(0, 200)), hfunc=hfunc.temp, h.prime=hprime.temp,
+ bvec_matrix=theta_matrix, link="continuation", EW_Fi.func=EW_Fi_MLM_func, 
+ delta0=1e-6, epsilon=1e-12, reltol=1e-6, delta=0.8, maxit=1000, random=TRUE, 
+ nram=1, rowmax=NULL, Xini=NULL, random.initial=TRUE, nram.initial=1)
\end{verbatim}

The sample-based EW D-optimal approximate design, denoted by $\boldsymbol{\xi}_{\rm SEW}$, is output below. 

\begin{verbatim}
> print(EW_forlion.design_MLM)

