\section{Primordial Black-Hole Mass Spectrum}
\label{sec:mass_spectrum}

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Mapping $k$-Modes to PBH Masses}{Mapping k-Modes to PBH Masses}}

For a horizon-reentry temperature $T_{\rm re}$ in the radiation era the
enclosed mass is
\begin{equation}
M_{\rm H}(T_{\rm re})
  \;\simeq\;30
  \!\left(\frac{T_{\rm re}}{10^{7}\,\mathrm{GeV}}\right)^{-2}M_\odot,
\label{eq:MHofT}
\end{equation}
assuming $g_{*}(T_{\rm re})\simeq106.75$ relativistic degrees of freedom
\cite{Carr1975}.  
Numerical collapse studies give the PBH mass as
$M_{\rm PBH}=\gamma\,M_{\rm H}$ with $\gamma\simeq0.2$ for radiation
backgrounds \cite{NiemeyerJedamzik1999}.  
Using $k=aH=1/\sqrt{2}\,a_{\rm re}t_{\rm re}$ and standard
radiation-dominated relations one obtains the convenient map
\begin{equation}
k(M_{\rm PBH})
  \;\simeq\;1.9\times10^{5}\,
  \gamma^{-1/2}
  \!\left(\frac{M_{\rm PBH}}{M_\odot}\right)^{-1/2}
  \,\mathrm{Mpc}^{-1},
\label{eq:k_M_relation}
\end{equation}
valid for $10^{-18}\,M_\odot\lesssim M_{\rm PBH}\lesssim10^{5}\,M_\odot$
\cite{GreenKavanagh2021}.  
Figure~\ref{fig:kM_map} visualizes this $k\!\leftrightarrow\!M$
correspondence, highlights astrophysically interesting mass scales, and
shows the equivalent horizon temperature on a secondary axis.
Throughout the remainder of the paper we set $\gamma=0.2$ when plotting
mass functions.

