The comparative analysis of the three models reveals important insights into both predictive performance and the quality of probability estimates. While LightGBM achieves the highest ROC-AUC at 0.743, its advantage over the generative model (0.742) is marginal and not practically significant. What makes the generative model particularly noteworthy is its ability to produce well-calibrated probabilities, as evidenced by its superior Balanced Brier Skill Score (BBSS) and the shape of its calibration curve, see Figure \ref{calibration_plots}. Although none of the models achieve perfect calibration, the generative model clearly demonstrates the most consistent and reliable probabilistic output. This performance is particularly impressive given the relatively simple distributional assumptions underlying the model, suggesting that a generative approach can still serve effectively as a classifier while providing meaningful probability estimates.

