\section{Model}
\label{sec:model}
An instance of a \textit{Matching Problem with Abandoment} is defined by the tuple $\mathcal{I} = \{\mathcal{T},\lambda,\mu, r \}$, where  $\m{T}$ is a finite set of agent types. 

{\bf Agent Arrival and Abandonment.}
Agents of type $i \in \m{T}$ arrive according to a Poisson process with rate $\lambda_i$. Arrivals are independent across types. For any time $t \ge 0$ and $i \in \m{T}$, let $\mathcal{A}_i(t)$ be the set of agents of type $i$ that arrive before time $t$. Let $\mathcal{A}(t) = \bigcup_{i \in \m{T}}\mathcal{A}_i(t)$. For each agent $a \in \mathcal{A}(t)$ , we let $i_a$ indicate the type of the agent, and $t_a$ indicate the agent's arrival time. The assumption of Poisson arrivals implies that conditioned on $|\mathcal{A}_i(t)|$, the times $\{t_a : a \in \mathcal{A}_i(t)\}$ are iid uniform on $[0,t]$. %\na{Are we using $i_a$ or $\tau_a$? Right now, inconsistent.}

%Agents of type $i$ arrive according to a Poisson process with rate $\lambda_i$. Arrivals are independent across types.  Each agent $a \in \mathcal{A}(t)$ is associated with a type $\tau_a \in \mathcal{T}$, an arrival time $t_a \in [0,t]$ and an unmatched departure time $d_a \ge t_a$. 

%For any time $t \ge 0$, let $\mathcal{A}(t) $ be the set of agents that arrive before time $t$, and let $\mathcal{A}_{i}(t)$ denote the set of agents of type $i$ that arrive before time $t$. The assumption of Poisson arrivals implies that for each $t$, the values $\{|\mathcal{A}_i(t)|\}_{i \in \mathcal{T}}$ are independent, $|\mathcal{A}_i(t)| \sim Pois(\lambda_i t)$, and conditioned on $|\mathcal{A}_i(t)|$, the times $\{t_a : a \in \mathcal{A}_i(t)\}$ are iid uniform on $[0,t]$. 

%Agents of type $i$ abandon at rate $\mu_i$. Formally, this means that for each $a \in \mathcal{A}_i(t)$, the maximum sojourn time $d_a - t_a$ is exponentially distributed with mean $1/\mu_{i}$, and these maximum sojourn times are drawn independently across agents.
Each agent $a \in \mathcal{A}_i(t)$ has a {\em maximum sojourn time} $s_a$: after waiting for $s_a$ periods, agent $a$ will depart unmatched. We assume that agents of type $i$ abandon the system at rate $\mu_i$. Formally, this means that the times $\{s_a\}_{a \in \m{A}_i(t)}$ are iid and exponentially distributed with mean $1/\mu_{i}$. We let $d_a = t_a + s_a$ be the time at which agent $a$ will depart unmatched.


{\bf Rewards.} 
We can conduct pairwise matches of agents. The reward earned from forming a match depends on the types of the agents involved. Say that a match has type $(i,j) \in \mathcal{T} \times \mathcal{T}$ if the earlier-arriving  agent in the match is of type $i$ and the later-arriving agent in the match is of type $j$. (Because arrivals follow a Poisson process, with probability one no two agents arrive at the exact same time.) Let $r_{ij} \in \mathbb{R}$ specify the reward from making an $(i,j)$ match. We generalize previous work by allowing $r_{ij}$ to be different from $r_{ji}$. %we allow $r_{ij}$ to be negative.%\footnote{In \Cref{sec:app:twotypes} we present a special case where assuming that $r_{ij} = r_{ji}$ can be used to show that greedy polices achieve half of omniscient in the special case where there are only two types of agents.}


