\section{Discussion}

%Greedy policies have been shown to perform  well in certain settings \citep{anderson2014dynamic, ashlagi2023matching}. \na{Check this reference, cite others?}

\label{sec:discussion}
In this work we presented an algorithm to find greedy policies that can guarantee at least half of the reward earned by the omniscient benchmark. While aspects of our approach parallel prior work, our key innovation lies in using a linear program ($\textup{LP}^\textup{ALG}$) that provides guaranteed {\em lower bounds} on policy performance. % rather than approximating theoretically optimal but practically unattainable match rates.

The most obvious limitation of our work is that we require strong assumptions on agents' departure rates. %(Our remaining results, which relate the value of our $\textup{LP}^\textup{ALG}$ to the omniscient benchmark, and the quantity $\mathbb{E}[N_i^\pi]$ to $\mathbb{P}(N_i^\pi > 0)$, are proved without these assumptions.)
We are uncertain whether these assumptions are truly necessary, and hope to extend our result for cases with general $\mu_i$. In particular, we present the following conjecture. 
\begin{conjecture}  \label{con:greedy}
    For any instance $\mathcal{I}$ our proposed greedy policy $\alg$  achieves at least half of the omniscient reward rate:
    \[V(\alg, \mathcal{I})\ge \frac{1}{2} \offopt.\]
\end{conjecture}
%\na{Do we want to strengthen this conjecture to ``our proposed greedy policy achieves at least half of the omniscient reward rate"? This is more specific/falsifiable.}
Because \Cref{thm:no-better-half} shows that a guarantee greater than $1/2$ is impossible for any online policy, this would imply that deterministic greedy policies suffice to get an optimal competitive ratio. The current limitation in extending of our approach lies in the fact that we don't know if $\PP{\sum_{i \in S} N_i^\pi > 0} \ge \gamma_S \sum_{i \in S} \mathbb{E}[N_i^\pi]$ holds for greedy policies. We know that this inequality does not hold for general Markovian policies, as shown in the example in \Cref{sec:app:alphasmaller}.  



