\section{Introduction}
Sound Source Localization (SSL) focuses on estimating the direction of arrival (DoA) of sound signals regarding the microphone array. This capability is crucial for many human-robot interaction (HRI) applications, including speech enhancement~\cite{enhance1}, speech separation~\cite{separation}, and speaker diarization~\cite{tao2021someone}. Traditionally, SSL has been approached as a signal-processing problem, such as the generalized cross-correlation with phase transform (GCC-PHAT) method~\cite{GCC}, multiple signal classification (MUSIC) algorithm~\cite{MUSIC}, and steered response power with phase transform (SRP-PHAT) method~\cite{SRPPHAT}. However, these conventional approaches perform suboptimally when dealing with multiple sound sources.

%These methods rely on analyzing the time differences of arrival (TA) between microphone pairs or evaluating spatial spectrum patterns to estimate sound source directions.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{intro-task.drawio.png}
    \caption{Comparison between conventional SSL and the proposed CI-AVL task: (a) Conventional SSL methods cannot associate predicted DoAs with specific sound source identities; (b) CI-AVL enables selective localization of the target sound source by aligning spatial features from mixed audio with semantic features from a visual prompt via a Semantic-Spatial Matching mechanism.}
\end{figure}

Researchers have increasingly explored deep neural network (DNN)-based methods to improve multi-source localization performance. Some frameworks integrate DNNs with classical techniques, using networks to refine peak detection~\cite{gccmlp,srp2} or subspace estimation~\cite{music1}, while others adopt end-to-end architectures to learn spatial representations directly from raw audio~\cite{cnndoa,cnnlstm_doa,seldnet,tfmamba}. Nevertheless, audio-only systems face inherent constraints: they fail to localize temporarily silent sources and exhibit degraded performance in noisy or reverberant environments. 

To overcome these limitations, researchers have introduced visual modality as a complementary cue, which provides persistent spatial contexts during acoustic silence. However, visual-only SSL methods are vulnerable to challenges such as illumination changes, occlusions, and limited field of view (FoV)~\cite{qianmulti}, where the audio cues can naturally compensate. In this context, audio and vision exhibit natural synergy: audio ensures continuous localization for occluded sources, while vision enhances robustness in acoustically adverse conditions~\cite{avmlp}.

Building on the complementarity of audio and visual modalities, various audio-visual localization (AV-SSL) approaches have been proposed to overcome unimodal limitations. These methods begin by extracting spatial features from both modalities, which are further combined through a fusion model. The fused features are then fed into a downstream network to predict the DoA \cite{qianmulti,avmlp,dgb,CMAF,lipnet,avseld,AVST,TAVF}.
Despite their effectiveness, a key challenge persists—the association of sound source identities. Existing methods can estimate DoA for all sound sources via audio-visual collaboration, but they struggle to selectively extract a specific target sound source of interest. Additionally, they rely heavily on paired audio-visual datasets, where the visual data explicitly corresponds to the sound source. These limitations hamper practical applications where isolating the target sound source is crucial.

%If the visual signal contains irrelevant information, it may even adversely degrade the localization performance.(先删掉)

The root of the identity association problem lies in a semantic-spatial misalignment. Previous methods align audio and vision only at the spatial level (“where the object is”), while neglecting semantic consistency (“what the object is”). However, human auditory perception follows a hierarchical process: we first semantically recognize what the sound source is, then spatially localize where it originates. This semantic grounding enables humans to selectively focus on the target sound source in a multi-source scenario, effectively solving the association problem.

% Semantic visual features primarily capture object identity ("what the object is"), while spatial audio features like GCC-PHAT, Inter-phase Difference (IPD), and Intensity Vectors (IV) primarily encode spatial location ("where the object is"). Directly concatenating or fusing these distinct representations, as done in prior methods, often leads to suboptimal performance due to modality misalignment. Human perception, however, follows a different process: we first recognize a sound source at a semantic level, then localize it at a spatial level. Inspired by human auditory perception, we propose a Semantic-Spatial Alignment mechanism, where the semantic representation of mixed audio serves as an intermediary to align visual semantic features with spatial audio features. 

Inspired by this cognitive process, we hypothesize that AV-SSL systems must bridge semantic and spatial representations to enable identity-aware localization. To this end, we introduce Cross-Instance Audio-Visual Localization (CI-AVL), a novel task that requires models to localize a specific target sound using a semantic visual prompt. As illustrated in Figure 1(b), the prompt is an image of a different instance from the same sound event category, providing semantic guidance to identify the target source. Unlike previous AV-SSL tasks that rely on paired audio-visual data, CI-AVL leverages category-level semantic visual information, significantly reducing dependence on manually curated datasets.

To tackle this challenging task, we propose VP-SelDoA, a framework designed to align and fuse heterogeneous modalities for selective localization. VP-SelDoA first performs a semantic-level modality fusion, integrating visual prompts with audio mixtures to identify the target’s semantic identity. It further employs a Frequency-Temporal ConMamba block to generate a target-selective mask that isolates the spatial cues of the desired sound source. To align heterogeneous semantic and spatial features, we introduce a Semantic-Spatial Matching mechanism, where the semantic representation of the mixed audio acts as an intermediary to align the visual prompt with spatial audio features. This alignment ensures consistent identity-location mapping, enabling precise extraction of the target’s DoA even in multi-source environments.

% A fundamental challenge in this approach is the inherent mismatch between semantic visual prompts and spatial audio features. Semantic visual features primarily capture object identity ("what the object is"), while spatial audio features like GCC-PHAT, Inter-phase Difference (IPD), and Intensity Vectors (IV) primarily encode spatial location ("where the object is"). Directly concatenating or fusing these distinct representations, as done in prior methods, often leads to suboptimal performance due to modality misalignment. Human perception, however, follows a different process: we first recognize a sound source at a semantic level, then localize it at a spatial level. Inspired by human auditory perception, we propose a Semantic-Spatial Alignment mechanism, where the semantic representation of mixed audio serves as an intermediary to align visual semantic features with spatial audio features.

To facilitate research on the CI-AVL task, we construct VGGSound-SSL, a large-scale dataset based on VGGSound~\cite{vggsound}, containing 39 hours of spatial audio paired with prompt images that encapsulate the semantic information of their corresponding sound events. Comprehensive experiments demonstrate that our approach achieves SOTA performance in the CI-AVL task. Our key contributions are summarized as:
\begin{itemize}
    \vspace{-1mm}
    \item We propose CI-AVL, a novel and challenging AV-SSL task that enables selective localization of a target sound source using a semantic visual prompt from a different instance within the same category. This formulation removes the reliance on explicitly paired audio-visual data, greatly improving practicality in real-world applications.

    \item We propose VP-SelDoA, a novel framework that addresses the identity-association problem in AV-SSL. It performs a semantic-level modality fusion and incorporates a Frequency-Temporal ConMamba module to generate a target-selective mask, enabling effective isolation of the desired sound.

    \item We introduce a Semantic-Spatial Matching mechanism to bridge the semantic-spatial gap. By aligning the visual prompt ("what") with spatial audio features ("where") through a semantic representation of the mixed audio, this human-inspired approach enables accurate extraction of spatial cues for the target source.

    \item We construct VGGSound-SSL, a large-scale AV-SSL dataset comprising 13,981 spatial audio clips across 296 sound event categories paired with semantic visual prompts. This dataset offers a valuable benchmark for future research in identity-aware localization.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\subsection{Audio-only SSL}

