Numerous DNN-based approaches have been developed to address the SSL problem. Some methods integrated traditional methods with neural network architectures. For instance, MLPGCC~\cite{gccmlp} used GCC-PHAT features as audio input, which were processed by a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) to estimate the DoA likelihood. DR-MUSIC~\cite{music1} improved the MUSIC algorithm by learning a better covariance matrix through a neural network. Cross3D~\cite{srp2} took the SRP-PHAT spatial spectrum as input and employed a causal 3D convolutional neural network (CNN) to locate sound sources.

Other approaches adopted fully neural architectures to estimate DoA. \cite{cnndoa} used the phase component of short-time Fourier transform (STFT) features as input and trained a CNN to extract spatial patterns from the feature maps. ~\cite{cnnlstm_doa} extended this approach by incorporating a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) module to enhance DoA estimation performance under noisy conditions. SELDNet~\cite{seldnet} utilized both the phase and magnitude components of STFT features and employed a Convolutional Recurrent Neural Network (CRNN) to learn high-level representations, achieving improved localization performance for unknown sound sources. More recently, TF-Mamba~\cite{tfmamba} replaced the LSTM block with a bidirectional Mamba~\cite{bimamba} architecture, enhancing sequence modeling and yielding improved DoA estimation in challenging acoustic environments.

Although these methods demonstrated strong performance on SSL tasks, they still faced several limitations. First, most models were unable to estimate the sound DoA during silent periods because their predictions relied on continuous acoustic cues. Second, their performance degraded significantly in highly noisy or reverberant environments, where spatial features such as GCC-PHAT became unreliable. While some approaches incorporated temporal modeling to increase robustness, they still lacked the capacity to generalize across diverse acoustic conditions.

\subsection{Audio-Visual SSL}
Researchers developed various audio-visual localization methods to leverage the complementary strengths of both audio and visual modalities. A variety of approaches used GCC-PHAT as input audio features while employing different techniques to encode visual information and fuse multimodal data. Early methods like MLP-AVC~\cite{qianmulti} encoded facial features as components of a multivariate Gaussian distribution and combined them with GCC-PHAT features, feeding into an MLP for localization. Building upon this framework, AVMLP~\cite{avmlp} introduced de-emphasis maps to suppress interference speakers, while DGB~\cite{dgb} employed a conditional variational auto-encoder (CVAE)~\cite{CVAE} to learn modality fusion and generate correlation functions for SRP-PHAT-based localization. CMAF~\cite{CMAF} proposed a cross-modal attentive fusion strategy, utilizing self-attention to capture intra-modal temporal dependencies and cross-attention to align audio and visual modalities. AVST~\cite{AVST} adopted dual Vision Transformer (ViT)~\cite{vit} models to process GCC-PHAT and visual features independently, concatenating their outputs for final DoA prediction.

Beyond GCC-PHAT, several studies explored alternative audio representations. ~\cite{MultiDoA} and ~\cite{TAVF} leveraged STFT features along with Gaussian-encoded visual information, integrating them through CNN or Transformer~\cite{transformer} encoders, respectively. ~\cite{lipnet} extracted lip features and combined them with audio filter bank (FBank) features through a trimodal gated fusion mechanism. AV-SELD~\cite{avseld} extracted Log-mel spectrograms and Intensity Vectors (IVs) from audio inputs, while processing visual information through ResNet~\cite{resnet}. These complementary features were processed by identical Conformer~\cite{conformer} networks and integrated through cross-modal attentive layers.

Despite effectiveness, these methods exhibit limitations. They rely on paired audio-visual data and are primarily designed for human speech, limiting their use for general sound sources. More importantly, they cannot associate estimated DoAs with corresponding sound sources in multi-source scenarios. This is because they mostly focus on spatial alignment between audio and visual features, while overlooking semantic information that characterizes sound source identities. Consequently, these methods are incapable of selectively isolating the desired sound sources.

\subsection{Summary}
Both audio-only and audio-visual SSL methods suffer from critical limitations. Audio-only methods struggle to locate sound sources during silent periods and are sensitive to noise and reverberation. On the other hand, audio-visual approaches rely heavily on paired audio-visual data and are primarily tailored to human speakers, limiting their scalability to real-world scenarios. Furthermore, these methods cannot associate estimated DoAs with specific sound source identities in multi-source environments, as they emphasize spatial alignment while neglecting semantic-level understanding. These challenges highlight the need for a semantically aware SSL framework capable of selectively extracting the target sound source.

\section{Methodology}
The architecture of our proposed VP-SelDoA is illustrated in Figure 2. The network consists of three phases: semantic-level modality fusion, target-selective masking, and DoA prediction. In this section, we first provide a formal problem formulation and then elaborate on each component of the VP-SelDoA.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
Let us start by introducing notation definitions. Unless stated otherwise, matrices are denoted in bold uppercase letters (e.g., $\mathbf{X}$, $\mathbf{Y}$), vectors in bold lowercase letters (e.g., $\mathbf{x}$, $\mathbf{y}$), and scalar variables in regular lowercase letters (e.g., $x$, $y$).

