For CI-AVL task, given the captured two-channel audio signals captured by a microphone pair ${\bf x}$ and a user-specified prompt image \( \bf I \), our goal is to predict the DoA of the image-aligned target sound event. We formulate this as a regression task, discretizing the DoA into 180 classes: \( \hat{\theta} = \{ j \mid 1 \leq j \leq 180, j \in \mathbb{Z} \} \). To better capture spatial continuity, we follow ~\cite{locselect} to model the posterior probability distribution of \( \hat{\theta} \) using a Gaussian-like vector \( p(\theta) \) instead of one-hot encoding:
% Since DoAs are spatially continuous, a one-hot encoding scheme may not effectively capture the inherent spatial relationships. Instead, we represent the posterior probability distribution of a sound event occurring in direction $\hat{\theta}$ using a Gaussian-like vector $p(\theta)$:
\begin{equation}
p(\theta) = \exp\left(-\frac{\left | \hat{\theta} - \theta \right | }{\sigma_{\theta}}\right)
\end{equation}
where $p(\theta)$ is centered on the ground truth $\theta$ with a standard deviation $\sigma_{\theta}$. The normalization factor $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_{\theta}}}$ is omitted as it does not affect the model's predictions.

