\subsection{Semantic-level Modality Fusion}
The misalignment between visual semantic and audio spatial features presents a major challenge for isolating target sound sources using visual cues. To address this, we introduce semantic audio features as a bridge between visual semantics and audio spatial information. By first fusing audio and visual inputs at the semantic level, our model identifies the target sound’s semantic attributes, which then guide the extraction of target-specific spatial features.

% This strategy leverages a multimodal fusion approach to align and integrate the semantic features of the mixed audio and the prompt image, thereby facilitating the extraction of target-specific semantic information from the mixed signal.

To achieve this, we use a pretrained audio encoder VGGish~\cite{vggish} to extract the semantic audio feature, denoted as ${{\bf F}_{A} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{A} \times t}}$, where $t$ represents the audio duration and $d_{A}$ the feature dimension. For the visual modality, we use a pretrained visual encoder CLIP~\cite{CLIP} to obtain the visual semantic feature ${\bf F}_{V} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{V}}$. To ensure compatibility with the audio sequence, the visual feature is expanded along the temporal dimension, resulting in ${\bf F}^{'}_{V} \in \mathbb{R}^{t \times d{V}}$.
${\bf F}_{A}$ and ${\bf F}^{'}_{V}$ are concatenated along the feature dimension to form a unified multimodal input sequence. The unified sequence is then processed by a Conformer network to output a semantic-aware multimodal prompt feature, ${\bf F}_{AV} \in \mathbb{R}^{(d_A+d_V) \times t}$, defined as
\begin{equation}
    {\bf F}_{AV} = \text{Conformer} \space ({\bf F}^{'}_{V} \textcircled{c} {\bf F}_{A})
\end{equation}
where $\textcircled{c}$ denotes concatenation along the feature dimension.

