\subsection{Target-selective Masking}
In this stage, we aim to generate a mask that selectively isolates the spatial information of the target sound event while suppressing interference sources. 
% We derived the real and imaginary components of the time-frequency (TF) domain signal, denoted as $X_{R}$ and $X_{I}$. 

We use the real components ${\bf X}_{R} \in \mathbb{R}^{2\times T \times F}$ of the STFT feature as the input (the imaginary component is denoted as ${\bf X}_{I}$). Prior work~\cite{tfgridnet} highlighted that spatial properties differ across frequencies. Narrow-band modeling captures frequency-specific features, but faces frequency permutation issues, whereas cross-band modeling ensures consistency across the spectrum~\cite{spatialnet}. Inspired by ~\cite{tfmamba}, our approach uses a Frequency-Temporal ConMamba block to integrate both narrow-band and cross-band modeling strategies, effectively capturing localized and globally coherent spatial features.

To further refine the extraction of target spatial information, we leverage the semantic-aware multimodal prompt feature ${\bf F}_{AV}$ by introducing a novel Semantic-Spatial Matching block, which aligns heterogeneous semantic and spatial features. The overall masking stage is organized into three modules: (1) a Frequency ConMamba module for processing spatial features independently across frequencies, (2) the Semantic-Spatial Matching module for feature alignment, and (3) a Temporal ConMamba module for refining spatial representations across time frames.

\noindent \textbf{Frequency ConMamba Layer} This module processes each frequency independently. Given an input ${\bf X}_{R} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 2 \times T \times F}$, where $B$ denotes the batch size and 2 represents the microphone numbers, we first apply a linear projection to eliminate the microphone dimension, resulting in a frequency-independent feature map ${\bf X}^{'}_{R} \in \mathbb{R}^{(B*F) \times T}$.

To effectively capture inter-frequency spatial characteristics, we employ a ConMamba layer~\cite{conmamba} block. As shown in Figure 2(a), the feature map is first processed by two parallel linear projections, producing ${\bf Z}_{1}$ and $\Delta_{1}$:
\begin{equation} {\bf Z}_{1} = \text{Linear}({\bf X}_{R}) \end{equation}
\begin{equation} \Delta_{1} = \text{SiLU}(\text{Linear}({\bf X}_{R}))\end{equation}
Here ${\bf Z}_1$ serves as the primary representation for sequence modeling, and $\Delta_1$ acts as a modulation factor for the sequence modeling output.

Subsequently, ${\bf Z}_{1}$ undergoes local feature extraction through a 1D convolution followed by a SiLU activation function. The resulting features are then processed by an SSM to capture long-range dependencies across frequencies. A LayerNorm (LN) is applied to normalize the output:
\begin{equation} {\bf Z}_{2} = \text{LN}(\text{SSM}(\text{SiLU}({\bf Z}_1))), \end{equation}

Finally, the sequence output ${\bf Z}_1$ is transformed by $\Delta_1$ through a element-wise multiplication to produce the final output ${\bf F}_{f}$:
\begin{equation} {\bf F}_{f} = \Delta_1 \otimes {\bf Z}_2, \end{equation}

\noindent \textbf{Semantic-Spatial Matching} This module aligns the semantic prompt feature ${\bf F}_{AV}$ with the spatial feature ${\bf F}_{f}$ to extract target-relevant spatial characteristics. As shown in Figure 2(b), it consists of two components: a Cross-Attention Conformer (CAC) block and a Self-Attention Conformer (SAC) block, designed for semantic-guided spatial feature refinement.

The CAC block uses the semantic prompt ${\bf F}_{AV}$ to selectively emphasize relevant spatial details in ${\bf F}_{f}$. A cross-attention mechanism is employed, where ${\bf F}_{AV}$ serves as the query, while ${\bf F}_{f}$ acts as the key and value:
\begin{equation}
    {\bf Q}^{\text{CAC}} = {\bf W}^{\text{CAC}}_{Q}F_{AV}; \space {\bf K}^{\text{CAC}} = {\bf W}^{\text{CAC}}_{K}F_{f}; \space {\bf V}^{\text{CAC}} = {\bf W}^{\text{CAC}}_{V}{\bf F}_{f},
\end{equation}
where ${\bf W}$s are learnable projection matrices. The attention mechanism then computes the weighted interactions:
\begin{equation}
    {\bf X}^{\text{CAC}} = \text{softmax}(\frac{{\bf Q}^{\text{CAC}}{\bf K}^{\text{CAC}^\top}}{\sqrt{d^{\text{CAC}}}}){\bf V}^{\text{CAC}},
\end{equation}
where $d^{\text{CAC}}$ is the feature dimension. The refined output ${\bf X}_{\text{CAC}}$ is further processed via a convolutional layer and a feed-forward network (FFN) to yield ${\bf X}^{'}_{\text{CAC}}$.

To further refine spatial-semantic alignment, the SAC block applies self-attention to ${\bf X}^{'}_{\text{CAC}}$, capturing intra-sequence dependencies. The input is projected into query, key, and value representations:
\begin{equation}
    {\bf Q}^{\text{SAC}} = {\bf W}^{\text{SAC}}_{Q} {\bf X}^{'}_{\text{CSC}}; {\bf K}^{\text{SAC}} = {\bf W}^{\text{SAC}}_{K}{\bf X}^{'}_{\text{CSC}}; {\bf V}^{\text{SAC}} = {\bf W}^{\text{SAC}}_{V}{\bf X}^{'}_{\text{CSC}},
\end{equation}
where ${\bf W}$ are learnable projection matrices. The self-attention computation follows:
\begin{equation}
    {\bf X}^{\text{SAC}} = \text{Softmax}(\frac{{\bf Q}^{\text{SAC}}{\bf K}^{\text{SAC}^\top}}{\sqrt{d^{\text{SAC}}}}){\bf V}^{\text{SAC}},
\end{equation}
where $d^{\text{SAC}}$ represents the dimensionality. The final feature ${\bf X}^{\text{SAC}}$ is processed through a convolutional layer and FFN, resulting in the enhanced spatially guided feature ${\bf X}^{'}_{\text{SAC}}$.

\noindent \textbf{Temporal Mamba Layer} 
The Temporal Mamba Layer processes time frames independently. It follows the same architecture as the Frequency Mamba Layer. The input ${\bf X}^{'}_{\text{SAC}}$ is first reshaped into ${\bf X}^{''}_{\text{SAC}} \in \mathbb{R}^{(B*T) \times F}$ before being processed by the ConMamba layer. The output is then passed through a MLP to generate a mask:
\begin{equation}
     {\bf F}_{Mask} = \text{MLP}(\text{ConMamba}({\bf X}^{''}_{\text{SAC}})),
\end{equation}

This mask is applied to ${\bf X}_{R}$ to produce the masked spectrogram ${\bf X}_{m}$:
\begin{equation}
    {\bf X}_m = {\bf X}_{R} \otimes {\bf F}_{mask}
\end{equation}
where $\otimes$ denotes element-wise multiplication. Finally, we concatenate the masked magnitude spectrogram ${\bf X}_{m}$ with the phase spectrogram ${\bf X}_{I}$ to obtain ${\bf X}_{clean}$, which serves as the input to the DoA Prediction Stage.

\subsection{DoA Prediction}
This stage estimates the DoA of the target sound using ${\bf X}_{clean}$ as input. An MLP layer with a Softmax function is applied to predict the DoA posterior probability:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{p}(\theta) = \text{Softmax}\left(\text{MLP}({\bf X}_{clean})\right )
\end{equation}
Finally, the target DoA is determined as the estimate through Eq.~\ref{eq:doa} with the maximum probability.
% \vspace{-3mm}
\subsection{Training Loss}
To optimize the time-frequency (TF) bin-level reconstruction between the masked spectrogram ${\bf X}_{clean}$ and the ground truth ${\bf X}_{gt}$, we adopt a Mean Square Error (MSE) loss:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{recon} = \left \| {\bf X}_{clean} - {\bf X}_{gt} \right \|^{2}_{2} 
\end{equation}

Additionally, a MSE loss is used for posterior probability-based DoA estimation, optimizing the predicted DoA distribution:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{DoA} = \sum_{\theta=1}^{180}\left \| \hat{p}(\theta)-p(\theta)\right \|^{2}_{2} 
\end{equation}

The final joint loss function is formulated as:
\begin{equation}
    \arg\min \mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{recon} + \mathcal{L}_{DoA}
\end{equation}

