\section{Experiments \& Discussions}
\subsection{Dataset Construction}
Table 1 offers an overview of current audio-visual datasets for localization tasks. Flickr-SoundNet~\cite{soundnet} and VGG-SS~\cite{VGG-SS}, offers bounding box annotations for visible sound sources, but are constrained by their mono audio format. In contrast, datasets like AV16.3~\cite{AV16.3}, CAV3D~\cite{cav3d}, SSLR~\cite{gccmlp}, and AVRI~\cite{CMAF} provide 3D location annotations captured in real indoor settings. However, their focus on human speech restricts their applicability to broader sound localization scenarios involving diverse sound events. 
Similarly, STARSS23~\cite{starss23} provides spatial audio recordings with position annotations for 13 different sound events, but predominantly contains multi-source recordings where human speech is often the dominant sound, making it less suitable for scenarios requiring clear sound event separation.

To address the scarcity of publicly available datasets for CI-AVL, we present VGGSound-SSL, a large-scale, and diverse dataset built upon VGGSound~\cite{vggsound}, enriched with spatial audio and prompt images spanning 296 sound event classes. This resource significantly advances comprehensive research on AV-SSL. As shown in Figure 3, the construction pipeline comprises two core components: spatial audio generation and prompt image generation.

\noindent\textbf{Spatial Audio Generation} We first extracted single-channel audio from each video and resample it to 16 kHz. To create realistic spatial audio, we convolved each audio segment with a random room impulse responses (RIR)~\cite{gpurir} to generate 2-channel spatial audio mixtures. To ensure diverse spatial conditions, we generated 10,000 RIRs by varying key parameters, including sound source location, room size, and reverberation time (T60). The detailed distribution of these parameters is presented in Table 2.

