\subsection{Baselines}
We compared our proposed method with SOTA audio-only and audio-visual baselines for DoA estimation. The audio-only baselines include 1) SELDNet~\cite{seldnet} and 2) GCC-MLP~\cite{gccmlp}, which estimate DoA using only the mixed spatial audio signals. For audio-visual methods, we evaluated 3) MLP-AVC~\cite{qianmulti}, 4) AVMLP~\cite{avmlp}, 5) DGB~\cite{dgb}, 6) AVST~\cite{AVST}, 7) AVSELD~\cite{avseld}, and 8) CMAF~\cite{CMAF} (The prompt image is encoded via their proposed visual encoder). 
To be mentioned, while AVMLP, DGB, and CMAF were originally designed for speaker tracking over time, we modified them to predict a single DoA instead of continuous tracking across time.

Additionally, we introduced an audio-visual baseline called VP-Conformer, which served as a variant of our proposed VP-SelDoA model. Specifically, VP-Conformer maintains the Semantic-Spatial Matching mechanism but replaces the Frequency-Temporal Mamba block with a Conformer architecture. This comparison allows us to evaluate the effectiveness of our architectural choices and determine whether the ConMamba block offers advantages over the Transformer-based model.
\vspace{-2mm}
\subsection{Training Setup}
All models were trained and evaluated on the VGGSound-SSL dataset. Each training sample was constructed by randomly selecting two audio segments from different sound events: one serving as the target source and the other as the interfering source. The two segments were mixed at an SNR of 0 dB. For visual prompting, we randomly selected an image corresponding to a different instance within the same sound category.

We trained our model using 140k steps with a batch size of 16. We used AdamW as the optimizer with a learning rate of 5e-3. For STFT computations, we set the frame size to 64 milliseconds and the hop size to 32 milliseconds.
\vspace{-2mm}
\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
We use MAE and ACC as the evaluation metrics (a lower MAE and a higher ACC signify superior performance).

MAE measures the absolute angular difference between the ground-truth DoA $\theta$ and the predicted DoA $\hat{\theta}$:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{MAE}(\downarrow) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\|\theta_n - \hat{\theta}_n\|
\end{equation}
where n is the sample index, and N is the total number of samples.

ACC measures the percentage of estimated DoA values falls within a predefined tolerance threshold $(\rho)$. In this study, $\rho=5^{\circ}$ (the same as \cite{avmlp,gccmlp}), indicating that predictions within ±5 degrees of the true value are considered correct. ACC is formulated as:
\begin{equation}
    \left.\mathrm{ACC}(\uparrow)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\sigma_n,\space\sigma_n=\left\{
\begin{array}
{l}1,\space\|\theta_n-\hat{\theta}_n\|\leq\rho \\
0, \space otherwise
\end{array}\right.\right.
\end{equation}
\vspace{-0.5mm}
\subsection{Results and Analysis}
Table 3 presents the results of our experiments conducted on the VGG-SSL test set across different SNRs. At SNR = 0 dB, our proposed VP-SelDoA outperforms other methods with the lowest MAE of $12.04^{\circ}$ and the highest ACC of 78.23\%. Notably, audio-only models achieved ACC close to 50\%. This performance aligns with expectations, as without visual cues, audio-only models randomly guess between two speakers, resulting in approximately 50\% accuracy. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of incorporating visual cues to selectively extract the desired sound sources. Furthermore, VP-SelDoA’s superior performance over competing audio-visual methods highlights the efficacy of our proposed Semantic-Spatial Matching mechanism.

% While the better performance of VP-SelDoA than other audio-visual methods further shows the superior alignment and fusion capabilities of our Semantic-Spatial Matching mechanism.
% Among audio-visual methods, AVSELD and CMAF demonstrate better performance than other audio-visual baselines, indicating that attention-based approaches integrate visual and audio features more effectively than direct concatenation techniques. 
% Compared to other audio-visual methods, our proposed VP-SelDoA achieved SOTA performance, showcasing the superior alignment and fusion capabilities of our Semantic-Spatial Matching mechanism. 
Moreover, VP-SelDoA surpasses VP-Conformer 
with a 5.92\% increase in ACC and a 3.73$^{\circ}$ reduction in MAE. 
% by 5.92\% higher ACC and $3.73^{\circ}$ lower MAE. 
This improvement can be attributed to the enhanced spatial information capture capabilities of the Frequency-Temporal ConMamba block.

To assess robustness under challenging noisy conditions, we conducted a noise generalization experiment. All models were trained on data with SNR = 0 dB and evaluated at lower SNR values ranging from -5 dB to -10 dB. This experiment examines the generalizability of each model when exposed to increasing levels of interference. As shown in Table 3, our proposed VP-SelDoA maintained superior performance compared to all baselines even under these adverse conditions, demonstrating its ability to capture comprehensive representations of target sound while effectively suppressing interference sounds. These results confirm the system's enhanced resilience in challenging acoustic environments.

% Effective feature extraction is essential for accurate sound source localization across diverse azimuth angles. 
Figure 4 presents a UMAP~\cite{umap} visualization of features from the penultimate layer of the DoA classifier. As shown in Figures 4(a) and 4(b), audio-only methods yield highly overlapping feature distributions, indicating poor discrimination between different DoA classes. In contrast, audio-visual methods show better class separation. Notably, features from our proposed VP-Conformer and VP-SelDoA exhibit tighter intra-class clustering and clearer inter-class boundaries, demonstrating superior discriminative capability over other audio-visual approaches.

