As shown in Table 4, our proposed Semantic-Spatial Matching mechanism outperforms these baselines, achieving gains of $8.17^{\circ}$ in MAE and 11.54\% in ACC. These improvements can be attributed to our design's effective integration of cross-attention and self-attention blocks, resulting in superior alignment performance.

\noindent\textbf{Multimodal Prompt}\space\space We further examined the impact of the semantic visual prompt and semantic audio feature on our method. As shown in Table 4, removing both components significantly degrades performance, with ACC dropping by 27.34\% and MAE increasing by $26.63^{\circ}$. This underscores the crucial role of external cues in isolating the target sound. When using only semantic audio features without the visual prompt, ACC declines by 10.28\% and MAE rises by $4.98^{\circ}$, highlighting the visual prompt’s importance in providing identity-specific guidance for selective isolation. Moreover, introducing semantic audio features as a bridge between visual semantics and spatial audio increases ACC by 13.32\% and reduces MAE by $9.07^{\circ}$. These results validate the effectiveness of our Semantic-Spatial Matching mechanism in aligning heterogeneous modalities.

Figure 6 visualizes model outputs under different prompt settings. In Figures 6(c) and 6(d), the absence of a visual prompt leads to ambiguous DoA estimates between the target and interference. In contrast, Figures 6(a) and 6(b) show that the visual prompt effectively directs the model’s attention toward the target. When both visual and semantic audio cues are present, the model produces a sharper, more confident DoA distribution centered on the target, demonstrating the strengths of our alignment strategy.

% \subsection{Limitations \& Future Work}
% Our proposed VP-SelDoA relies on estimating a sound event-dependent mask to selectively extract target sounds while filtering out interference and environmental noise. Although our experimental results demonstrate that this approach is effective, applying masks to spectrograms inevitably causes some degree of spatial information loss. This information loss can lead to degraded DoA estimation accuracy. Future work should focus on developing more sophisticated masking strategies that minimize this oversuppression problem, or alternatively, explore novel methodologies that can achieve selective sound source localization without relying on masking mechanisms at all. Such advances could potentially preserve critical spatial cues while still effectively isolating target sound sources in complex acoustic environments.

\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we introduce CI-AVL, a novel and challenging task that locates target sound source using image prompts from different instances within the same sound event category. To tackle this task, we propose VP-SelDoA, a framework that leverages semantic visual prompts to generate target-selective masks to isolate target sound among multiple sound sources. We further devise a Semantic-Spatial Matching mechanism to effectively align heterogeneous semantic and spatial features. To support research in this area, we build VGG-SSL, a large-scale dataset comprising 13,981 spatial audio clips paired with semantic prompt images across diverse sound categories. Extensive experiments show that our method outperforms SOTA approaches, achieving a MAE of $12.04^{\circ}$ and an ACC of 78.23\%.

