It is important to note that while low entropy may reflect population-level convergence on a trait, this does not necessarily indicate psychological homogeneity at the individual level. Rather, it reflects a compression of response diversity which may result from cultural, developmental, or ecological influences. Phenomenologically, this is in line with what would be expected from a dynamic system.

\subsection{Relevance of Shannon Entropy to Behavioral Trait Expression in a Population}

Entropy provides a complementary tool to variance decomposition. While heritability estimates describe the proportion of variance explained by genetics, entropy characterizes the structure of that variance across categorical trait levels. Entropy dynamics over time can detect the emergence of trait stratification, or adaptive convergence. Sudden shifts or non-monotonic entropy trends may indicate environmentally-driven phase transitions (such as bifurcations) or the breakdown of prior genetic-environmental equilibria, all of which work in favor of dynamical systems understandings \cite{strogatz2014}.

By translating the population-level psychometric Likert-scale data into Shannon entropy, the investigator gains a robust, interpretable, and mathematically principled way to monitor the collective distributional dynamics of traits over time. This serves as a useful companion to statistical tools in psychology and behavioral genetics, especially when applied in conjunction with nonlinear dynamics, stability analysis, and entropy-based modeling frameworks.

The method and approach of the ODEs were devised as a way of modeling personality trait dynamics within a 'psychometric ecosystem'. The argument is that if psychological traits are considered as dynamical states with nested relative dynamical points of chaotic stability (multistable attractors points where systems tend), then traits like neuroticism, which are currently treated as maladaptive, may more accurately be considered as dynamically evolving states nested within a complex system. This model sets up future inquiry of the genomic concept by starting at the ‘layer’ of pyschological data.  With this consideration, information compression is arguably justified in that, while a relatively small sample size, n=7, each data point represents a high density of information as each node consists of a subject pool n~1,700 at survey start, with five associated variations per question, all of which were considered six times over more than twenty years. This is rich information density per point.

\subsection{Comparable Approaches}

Several approaches have been proposed to model the evolution or persistence of psychological traits, but leveraging formal dynamical systems rooted in systems biology is relatively rare. Traditional behavioral approaches, such as those found behavioral genetics or psychology toolsets focus on variance decomposition and heritability estimates derived from twin or family designs, as well as on qualitative psychological assessments, respectively. These models can be statistically powerful, as well as analytically informative, but typically lack recursive temporal dynamics which embed the system-under-review in a realistic environment. A lack of such environmental considerations can at times lead to replicative difficulties \cite{duncan2019, osc2015}, particularly in behavioral and psychiatric research, where trait expression is highly context-sensitive.

In psychology, dynamic systems theory has occasionally been used to describe developmental trajectories or emotional regulation patterns \cite{vangeert1991, granic2003}, but these implementations typically rely on qualitative descriptions, symbolic representations, or low-dimensional visualizations rather than formal differential modeling frameworks grounded in biological constraints.

Additionally, psychophysics, which, "usually leads to some mathematical representation of mapping from inputs to outputs, in which the environment is given and the behavior is dependent and the mapping is written outside time, being static and not dynamic," has seen approaches dating back to at least 1860, with Gustav Fechner's "Elements of Psychophysics' \cite{gregson2008}. Gregson, 2008, gives a good overview on psychophysics, as well as their own approach developed beginning in 1988. Gregson's approach uses a discrete-time, complex cubic recursion but in contrast to the approach developed in this paper, their approach is phenomenological, not biologically driven, with chaotic structure emerging from iteration in the complex plane (the real part of the equation mapping to observable response; the imaginary part of the equation maps to internal fluctuation). Additionally, Gregson's approach can be considered as more theoretical than empirical.

While traditional psychophysics approaches are subject to classic arguments between physicists and psychologists "about the measurability of sensation... and about the additivity or nonadditivity of levels of reported sensation... what is actually done in psychophysics is to match judgments, of magnitude or probability, made in various forms of choice or discrimination against increments of stimulus activity levels, usually of intensity or duration," \cite{gregson2008}.  This is on a different scale of domain size and rigor than the approach which this paper develops.

Likewise, a conceptually similar but ontologically distinct avenue of approach can also be found in frameworks for collective intelligence, such as Sulis, 2008 \cite{sulis2008}. While considering the population level domain, the approach of Sulis (2008) is focused more on emergent behavior exhibited by social insects rather than human or animal psychological traits.

Regarding the use of Shannon entropy, entropy and dynamical systems have been explored in behavioral science using approaches such as state space grids \cite{lewis1999, hollenstein2004}, Markovian transition models \cite{bakeman1997}, and orbital decomposition \cite{guastello1998}. These methods provide valuable insights into real-time interaction patterns, symbolic dynamics, and entropy-based measures of coherence and Pincus gives a solid overview of these approaches while addressing the philosophical question of "what is psychotherapy?" \cite{pincus2008} and addressing 'metaorder' in psychotherapeutic systems \cite{tschacher1998}. However, the aforementioned entropy and meta-system acknowledging approaches lack a formal biological or evolutionary basis, and do not integrate entropy as a functional input into continuous-time models. In contrast, the present framework treats entropy not only as a descriptive measure, but as a compressed state variable embedded within a system of biologically grounded differential equations.    

In terms of biological systems modeling, work by Kaneko and collaborators \cite{kaneko2006,kaneko2013}, applies nonlinear ODEs to gene regulatory networks and cell differentiation. These models demonstrate how stability and bifurcation emerge at the biological level from feedback and constraint structures--the same principles this paper analyzes in the population level domain of behavioral traits.

To the author's knowledge, no published model has unified these approaches by treating psychometric trait evolution as a recursive, entropy-driven dynamical system informed by biological constraint terms. The following presented framework seeks to bridge this gap.

To sum up: 'Psychophysics'-style models typically focus on sensory-cognitive dynamics within abstract or complex-valued recursive maps (Gregson); collective intelligence approaches work on population-level domains but with differing analytical focus; Shannon entropy-assessing models have been developed but limited to the realm of discrete-analysis, or nonbiological, theoretic-minded models, or psychotherapeutic models; and existing biological systems modeling is currently focused in the realms of gene regulatory networks \cite{kaneko2013}, as well as bifurcation-driven transitions and slow-control variables in pattern formation and developmental trajectories \cite{kohsokabe2021}. 

This positions the following approach developed in this paper as intellectually adjacent, but more biologically integrated, empirically grounded, more general, and more extendable than similar but ontologically distinct approaches. It can be considered that the approached developed here is a natural follow up to the preceding approaches, as qualitative visualization and symbolic entropy, while both previously utilized, are here extended to nonlinear differential modeling grounded in dynamical biological considerations. 

\section{Methods for the Proposed Ordinary Differential Equation System}

The following section proposes and defines a system of coupled nonlinear differential equations to model the evolution of psychometric traits in a population, incorporating mutation, selection, pleiotropy, and environmental feedback. The variables, in current form, represent population-level entropy-derived metrics of trait expression over time. The models are set up, currently, to assess a phenotypic behavioral trait as it may be expressed in the entropy of the 'psychometric ecosystem'.

This system introduces an emergent feedback mechanism: traits influence stress, which feeds back into metabolic cost, which constrains trait persistence. The system is recursive, entropy-driven, and sensitive to parameter variation. This enables analysis of bifurcation, stability, and long-term trait evolution under realistic biological constraint conditions.

\subsection{Selection Pressure Dynamics}

\begin{equation}
\frac{dN}{dt} = \mu N - s(N) \cdot N, \quad \text{where} \quad s(N) = \alpha \cdot N
\end{equation}

This equation models the dynamics of neuroticism-like traits \( N \), where the trait increases via mutation (\( \mu N \)) but is constrained by a selection cost that scales with trait expression amounting, essentially, to: (\( \alpha N^2 \)) but left in general form, above. 

This portion of the model creates a natural self-limiting mechanism similar to logistic growth but biologically interpreted as fitness cost accumulation. This argues that a trait such as neuroticism grows via mutation but may stabilize in a population as part of a self-limiting process due to selection-cost.

\subsection{Pleiotropic Trait Dynamics}

\begin{equation}
\frac{dP}{dt} = \mu P - \beta P \cdot \left( \frac{E_{\text{metabolic}}}{G} \right)
\label{eq:pleiotropy}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
E_{\text{metabolic}} = c_1 P + c_2 N + c_3 E_{\text{stress}}
\label{eq:emetabolic}
\end{equation}

Here, \( P \) represents pleiotropic traits that carry nontrivial metabolic costs. The term \( \mu P \) represents mutational introduction of new traits, while the second term represents suppression via metabolic constraints. The effective energy cost \( E_{\text{metabolic}} \) is composed of additive burdens from pleiotropy, neuroticism, and stress exposure, normalized by total metabolic capacity \( G \). This is interpretable as: more traits = more energy costs = more suppression of trait expression.

The inclusion of metabolic factors is inspired by ecological systems approaches, including (but ontologically distinct from): Dynamical Energy Budget theory (DEB) and the West, Brown, Enquist model \cite{kearney2012}, as well as by systems genetic approaches such as Civelek \& Lusis (2014) \cite{civelek2014}

This pleiotropic term is here modeled heuristically at the level of population level psychometric data, so traits such as 'hot-headedness' may be treated by the ODE system as a putative 'Neurotic' trait while a trait such as 'tendency for worry' may be heuristically assessed as an associated pleiotropic trait. This approach is based on behavioral genetical analytic approaches, such as Blizard et al. (2023) who utilize Maudsley rat and Genome Wide Associated Studies (GWAS) to link neuroticism with conditions like Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) and anxiety \cite{blizard2023}.  While the approach put forth in this paper is heuristic and currently limited to population level psychometric data, the chosen data points for Neurotic-like traits were carefully associated with biologically realistic potential pleiotropic traits.

While Blizard et al. do not directly frame generational neurotic recurrence as 'stability', per se, their findings suggest that the persistence of neuroticism is regulated by both genetic and environmental factors, which in turn maintains the trait's expression across generations.

\subsection{Environmental Stress Dynamics}

\begin{equation}
\frac{dE_{\text{stress}}}{dt} = \gamma E_{\text{stress}} \cdot \left( \frac{N}{N + K} \right)
\label{eq:stress}
\end{equation}

Stress levels \( E_{\text{stress}} \) are modeled as increasing due to neuroticism expression, modulated by a saturation constant \( K \) to prevent unbounded growth. This form reflects a positive feedback loop: as neurotic traits accumulate, individuals may generate or perceive more environmental stress, reinforcing trait expression indirectly via pleiotropic energy cost coupling. While typically it is expected that environment influences the trait, this system argues that both influence each other in an emergent, recursive feedback loop dynamic.

\subsection{Model Parameters and Definitions}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \( N(t) \): Neuroticism-like trait frequency (entropy-normalized)
    \item \( P(t) \): Expression level of pleiotropic traits
    \item \( E_{\text{stress}}(t) \): Environmental stress level
    \item \( \mu \): Mutation rate (applies to both \( N \) and \( P \))
    \item \( \alpha \): Selection cost scaling factor (for \( N \))
    \item \( \beta \): Pleiotropic energy penalty scaling factor
    \item \( \gamma \): Stress amplification rate
    \item \( c_1 \): Energy cost per unit of \( P \)
    \item \( c_2 \): Energy cost per unit of \( N \)
    \item \( c_3 \): Stress-induced metabolic burden factor
    \item \( G \): Total metabolic capacity (e.g., bounded resource constraint)
    \item \( K \): Saturation constant controlling nonlinear stress feedback
\end{itemize}

 The term \(\frac{E(G)}{G}\) represents the metabolic cost of expressing these traits, where \(E(G)\) is the energy expenditure and \(G\) is the available metabolic capacity. In the initial model of psychometric data, \(G\) is set equal to 1.

Equations can be utilized individually, or coupled; for traits as demonstrated here, or in similar but distinct scenarios.  While the general dynamic is represented here, the above example also invokes a ‘Trait Frequenecy Dynamics’ approach. Additional approaches could see comparing High Anxiety strains vs Low Anxiety strains of a specified gene, with multiple neurotic or pleiotropic terms comparatively analyzed, for instance.

