\section{Results}

\subsection{Shannon Entropy Profiles of Trait Dynamics}

Trait-wise, of the initially analyzed 7 traits, entropy values revealed non-random but nonlinear evolution across the measured decades. With the notable exception of 'satisfaction', entropy decreased for all measured traits from 1984 through 2007, implying a transient phase of convergence for most of the measured traits, but with notable divergence in the satisfaction measures of the population-level distribution.  While these psychometric traits are not biological data, per se, the measured entropy was here assessed as representative of a sort of biologically-determined global system 'curvature' (drawing to mind the geometry of dynamical systems), and the entropy measures were utilized in service of analyzing and tuning the ODE model. 

\subsection{Dynamical Model Fit}

The system of nonlinear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) described in the previous section was numerically fit to the normalized Shannon entropy data using constrained optimization via the L-BFGS-B algorithm (as inspired by machine learning approaches to ODE optimization) \cite{liu1989}. Ratio based normalization was applied to entropy bit-values, in line with Multi-omics Batch Corrective Approaches \cite{zheng2024}. Parameter values were held constant across both subsystems ($N$ and $P$) to preserve internal consistency. 

Note: while L-BFGS-B optimization was used on initial runs of the data, the iterations below are optimized, rather, for visual and theoretic demonstrative purposes, while remaining within realistic bounds.  As such, these models should be treated as illustrative of procedure more so than with regards to the analysis of the data itself. Optimized data, analyzed elsewhere, has so far demonstrated the same tendencies, but further empirical testing is in progress outside of this pre-print.

The following parameter set yielded initial \textit{best-fit} results for \textit{Hot-Tempered} as the \(N\) term and \textit{Worry} as the associated \(P\) term:

\noindent\textbf{First parameter set:}
\[
\alpha = 0.008, \quad \mu = 0.00001, \quad \beta = 0.16031, \quad \gamma = 0.03, \quad c_1 = 2.0, \quad c_2 = 0.21, \quad K = 0.5,
\]

with \(c_3\) initially set to 0.

To explore these initial parametric settings, only the \(\alpha\) and \(\beta\) terms were perturbed, with increase in \(\alpha\) representative of stronger natural selection against high levels of the neurotic proxy trait; and the relatively smaller increase in \(\beta\) as representative of energy availability stabilizing as though around an attractor. This combination has the overall effect of simulating a neurotic trait being directly selected against while a broader constellation of traits such as 'Worry' don't face the same pressures. This sees the pleiotropic-proxy trait as conditionally tolerated under 'current conditions'.

